      "data": {
        "text": "**Defining the Code's Purpose**\n\nI'm starting to formalize the plan for `aggregate_metric_stats.py`. Right now, my focus is setting a well-defined purpose, so that I understand exactly what functionality will be contained in the code. I am thinking of a very concrete external provider to test the function with.\n\n\n**Outlining the Structure**\n\nNow I'm focusing on the code structure for `aggregate_metric_stats.py`. I've decided to use Google Cloud Monitoring, so I'm importing `google.cloud.monitoring_v3`. The main function will be `get_aggregated_metric_stats`, taking project ID, metric type, and a time window as inputs. Inside, I'll instantiate `MetricServiceClient` and construct the project name and time interval objects, ready for the query.\n\n\n**Defining the Implementation**\n\nNow I'm diving deeper into the specifics. I'm choosing Google Cloud Monitoring and the `google-cloud-monitoring` library. The Python file, `aggregate_metric_stats.py`, will define a function that will take a project ID, metric type, and time window (start and end times) as input. I'm integrating the `google.cloud.monitoring_v3` module and making a `MetricServiceClient` instance. I'm focusing on outlining the steps: import modules, define the function with input parameters, create the `MetricServiceClient`, construct the project name, define the time interval, define aggregation parameters, call the `list_time_series` method, and process/return the data. I'm planning to write the code directly.\n\n\n",
        "time": {
          "end": 1789867516039,
          "start": 1789867512005
        },
        "type": "reasoning"
      },
      "id": "prt_0bc6ad0c50013I3yYOHzIGQLJw",
